On Wed, Aug 19, 2026 at 8:47 AM Mathew Wedel <mathew.wedel@gmail.com> wrote:

> Would the ribs be “girdled” in life by gastralia in any way?
>>
>
> Depends on what you mean. The ribs would have been tethered to the sternum
> by sternal ribs, which are ossified in birds but cartilaginous in most
> other critters. IIRC it is now thought that the supposed gastralia of
> Brontosaurus yahnapin were actually sternal ribs. Which leaves us I think
> with just about zero good cases of ossified gastralia in sauropods.
> Homologous structures may have been present as tendons or cartilage.
>
> As far as "girdled", the sternal ribs provide a direct mechanical linkage
> between the distal ends of the dorsal ribs (at least the first few) and the
> sternal plates. The gastralia sit caudal to the sternal ribs, embedded in
> the abdominal wall muscles. The ribs are only constrained by the gastralia
> in that all of these bones are embedded in the wall of the torso, and the
> ventral profile of the torso has to meet up with the sternal plates
> anteriorly and the distal ends of the pubes posteriorly, because those are
> the skeletal anchors for the body wall muscles.
>
> Hope this helps!
>
> Best,
>
> Matt
